    content: `
<p class="lead">Medical practice reception design must simultaneously address requirements specific to healthcare — patient confidentiality at the counter, infection control, patient anxiety management, and efficient clinical workflow — while creating a welcoming environment that reduces rather than increases patient stress. These requirements produce design decisions that differ significantly from corporate reception design.</p>

<h2>Patient Privacy at Reception</h2>
<p>Medical reception counters must provide acoustic privacy for patient conversations — the presenting patient should not be overheard by others in the waiting area. Solutions include: raising the counter height (100mm above standard to reduce sound transmission), installing acoustic screens or glass panels, positioning the counter away from the waiting area, and using sound masking systems in the waiting zone. The Privacy Act's requirements for health information privacy extend to the physical reception environment.</p>

<h2>Infection Control Requirements</h2>
<p>Healthcare environments require surface materials that support the infection control protocols of the practice. Key requirements: non-porous surface materials on reception counters, seating, and waiting area furniture (vinyl upholstery, laminate surfaces, and powder-coated metal rather than porous fabric and timber); cleanability with healthcare-grade disinfectants (most commercial furniture fabrics are incompatible with bleach-based cleaners); and the elimination of horizontal dust-trapping surfaces in the waiting area (avoid plant arrangements and decor with complex geometry).</p>

<h2>Patient Seating Configuration</h2>
<p>Medical waiting room seating must accommodate: patients with mobility limitations (chair height 430–460mm, with armrests for push-up support), diverse body sizes (seating width of 500mm+ minimum), patients with anxiety (seating that provides some separation from strangers — not uniform bench rows), and companion waiting (seating arranged to allow two people to sit together). Anti-bacterial or antimicrobial seat coverings are recommended for high-volume practices.</p>

<h2>Paediatric Zones</h2>
<p>General practice and paediatric settings benefit from a defined children's zone in the waiting area: lower seating for children, a play table or activity board, children's books and activities, and positioning that allows parent supervision while seated in adjacent adult seating. This zone serves the practical management function of concentrating children's activity in a defined area while also demonstrating the practice's consideration for the family experience.</p>

<h2>Wayfinding and Communication</h2>
<p>Medical reception areas benefit from clear and comprehensive wayfinding: room numbering or naming systems that allow patients to navigate to consulting rooms without assistance, estimated wait time displays (digital or whiteboard) that manage patient expectations, and clear signage for amenities (bathrooms, accessibility facilities). Reducing the need for patients to approach reception with wayfinding questions reduces queue pressure and improves efficiency.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Design Your Practice Reception</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk designs and supplies healthcare reception furniture for Australian medical practices. Request a consultation.</p>
</div>
`,
